{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Biological evolution has no conscious goal of error elimination; Popperian science does.","Survival in nature may involve luck; survival in severe testing is meant to be logically rigorous.","Evolution accumulates changes; Popperian science aims to refute and replace."],"overturn_conditions":["If biological survival is shown to imply probabilistic future fitness, the analogy supports induction.","If scientific testing cannot be decoupled from expectations of future performance."],"shared_structure":["Selection operates on survival of the fittest (or most tested), not on the probability of future survival.","Preference for the survivor is a heuristic for continued testing, not a guarantee of adaptation.","The process eliminates the unfit but does not identify the optimal."],"source_memory_refs":["evolutionary_selection_pressure"],"transfer_claims":["Just as we bet on the organism that survived a plague without assuming it is immune to the next one, we bet on the corroborated theory without assuming it is probable.","The 'fitness' of a theory is its testability, not its truth."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms as a non-inductive methodological rule of 'critical rationality,' but accepting this defense costs the scientist any claim to predictive reliability or increasing truth-probability, reducing the preference to a purely procedural commitment to keep error-elimination active.","counterconditions":["If 'severity of test' implicitly assumes that passing such a test makes future passing more likely, then the definition of severity smuggles in induction.","If the choice to act on the better-corroborated theory is based on any expectation of practical success rather than mere procedural consistency, it collapses into pragmatic induction.","If Popper cannot distinguish his 'methodological rules' from the 'conventionalism' he rejected without appealing to the utility (and thus implied reliability) of those rules, the distinction fails."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The argument distinguishes between 'justification' (which implies induction) and 'rational preference' (which implies choosing the theory that has best withstood attempts at falsification). The mechanism is a disjunctive syllogism applied to methodology: since we must act, and since unfalsified theories are not equal in their exposure to risk, we prefer the one that has survived the severest tests. This is not an inference that the future will resemble the past (induction), but a decision to subject our current best guess to the highest possible risk of immediate refutation. The 'cost' is that this preference offers no guarantee of success, no probability assignment, and no assurance that the surviving theory is closer to the truth than a randomly chosen unfalsified one; it only assures us that we are following the most rigorous procedure for error detection available.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}